<main id="main">

<section class="dn-hero"><div class="dn-hero-g"><div class="dn-hero-t"><p class="dn-ey">Tonight&rsquo;s dinner, decided</p><h1>The main is easy. It is everything around it that needs a plan.</h1><p>Dinette helps with the parts of dinner nobody writes recipes for: what to serve alongside, how much to cook and what to do with what is left.</p><p class="dn-acts"><a class="btn" href="#tools">Plan tonight</a><a class="dn-a" href="side-dishes-guide.html">How to choose sides</a></p></div><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1755811248324-c70c1f10a7fd?auto=format&fit=crop&w=900&h=700&q=75" alt="people sharing a variety of dishes at a round table" width="900" height="700" fetchpriority="high"></figure></div></section>

<section class="dn-s"><div class="dn-w"><ol class="dn-steps"><li><span>1</span><div><h3>Choose the main</h3><p>Whatever you fancy or have. That is the only decision made on appetite.</p></div></li><li><span>2</span><div><h3>Balance it</h3><p>Add one starchy side and one vegetable that contrast with the main.</p></div></li><li><span>3</span><div><h3>Cook the right amount</h3><p>Enough for tonight, plus a planned portion for tomorrow if you want one.</p></div></li></ol></div></section>

<section class="dn-s dn-butter" id="tools"><div class="dn-w"><p class="dn-ey">Dinner tools</p><h2>Three decisions, made for you</h2>
<div class="dn-tool"><div class="dn-tool-a"><span class="dn-no">No. 1</span><h3>Side Dish Matcher</h3><p>Pick the main and the mood. Get two sides and a finishing touch.</p><label for="sd-m">The main is<select id="sd-m"><option value="chicken">Roast or grilled chicken</option><option value="beef">Steak or roast beef</option><option value="fish">Fish</option><option value="pasta">A pasta dish</option><option value="curry">A curry or stew</option><option value="veg">A vegetable bake or tart</option></select></label><label for="sd-k">I would like dinner to feel<select id="sd-k"><option value="light">Light and fresh</option><option value="cosy">Warm and comforting</option></select></label></div><div class="dn-tool-b" id="sd-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<div class="dn-tool"><div class="dn-tool-a"><span class="dn-no">No. 2</span><h3>Leftover Remix Finder</h3><p>Tell it what is in the fridge.</p><label for="lr-l">I have leftover<select id="lr-l"><option value="chicken">Roast chicken</option><option value="rice">Cooked rice</option><option value="roastveg">Roast vegetables</option><option value="mash">Mashed potato</option><option value="pasta">Cooked pasta</option><option value="mince">Chilli or bolognese</option></select></label></div><div class="dn-tool-b" id="lr-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
<div class="dn-tool"><div class="dn-tool-a"><span class="dn-no">No. 3</span><h3>Dinner Quantity Calculator</h3><p>How much to buy for the people at the table.</p><div class="dn-2"><label for="dq-a">Adults<input id="dq-a" type="number" min="0" max="40" value="4" inputmode="numeric"></label><label for="dq-c">Children<input id="dq-c" type="number" min="0" max="40" value="2" inputmode="numeric"></label></div><label for="dq-m">Main course<select id="dq-m"><option value="bone">Meat on the bone</option><option value="boneless" selected>Boneless meat or fish</option><option value="pasta">Pasta as the main</option><option value="rice">A rice dish as the main</option></select></label></div><div class="dn-tool-b" id="dq-o" aria-live="polite"></div></div>
</div></section>

<section class="dn-s"><div class="dn-w"><p class="dn-ey dn-c">The idea behind a good plate</p><h2 class="dn-c">Contrast in three directions</h2><div class="dn-tri"><article><h3>Texture</h3><p>Soft needs crisp. A stew wants crusty bread or a crunchy slaw; a crisp schnitzel wants something yielding, like mash.</p></article><article><h3>Richness</h3><p>Rich needs sharp. Creamy, cheesy or fatty mains are lifted by lemon, vinegar, pickles or bitter leaves.</p></article><article><h3>Temperature</h3><p>Hot welcomes cool. One cold or room-temperature dish makes a plate more interesting and the cooking easier.</p></article></div></div></section>

<section class="dn-s dn-olive"><div class="dn-w dn-mos"><figure class="dn-m1"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1792426442177-99ef44ada6f9?auto=format&fit=crop&w=440&h=520&q=75" alt="plate of white rice topped with avocado and prawns" width="440" height="520" loading="lazy"></figure><div><p class="dn-ey">A habit worth having</p><h2>Cook once, eat twice</h2><p>The easiest dinner of the week is the one that was half made yesterday. When you roast a chicken, roast two trays of vegetables. When you cook rice, cook double and chill half quickly. Tomorrow&rsquo;s dinner is then a matter of assembly.</p><p>The trick is to plan the second meal as something different, so it does not feel like the same dinner again.</p></div><figure class="dn-m2"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1762631882052-bb8aea869735?auto=format&fit=crop&w=440&h=520&q=75" alt="sizzling prawns and vegetables in a cast-iron pan" width="440" height="520" loading="lazy"></figure></div></section>

<section class="dn-s"><div class="dn-w"><p class="dn-ey">Read these</p><h2>The two guides</h2><div class="dn-gds"><a href="side-dishes-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1771161409331-39b927fe6f81?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=420&q=75" alt="plate of carved turkey on a table" width="640" height="420" loading="lazy"><div><span>Guide one</span><h3>The Side Dishes Guide</h3><p>How to choose sides by contrast, with pairings for every kind of main and sides that can be made ahead.</p></div></a><a href="leftovers-guide.html"><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1738717222021-a70de9540efc?auto=format&fit=crop&w=640&h=420&q=75" alt="person standing over a table filled with food" width="640" height="420" loading="lazy"><div><span>Guide two</span><h3>The Leftovers Guide</h3><p>Cooling, storing and reheating safely, and turning yesterday&rsquo;s dinner into something new.</p></div></a></div></div></section>

<section class="dn-s dn-butter"><div class="dn-w dn-qt"><div><p class="dn-ey">Shopping list numbers</p><h2>How much per person</h2><p>These are raw weights for an adult with an ordinary appetite. Halve them for young children, and add a quarter for teenagers.</p></div><div class="tblw"><table class="dn-tbl"><thead><tr><th>Food</th><th>As a main</th><th>As a side</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Boneless meat or fish</td><td>150&ndash;180 g</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr><tr><td>Meat on the bone</td><td>300&ndash;350 g</td><td>&mdash;</td></tr><tr><td>Dried pasta</td><td>90&ndash;100 g</td><td>60 g</td></tr><tr><td>Dried rice</td><td>75 g</td><td>50 g</td></tr><tr><td>Potatoes</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>200&ndash;250 g</td></tr><tr><td>Vegetables</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>120&ndash;150 g</td></tr><tr><td>Salad leaves</td><td>&mdash;</td><td>40 g</td></tr></tbody></table></div></div></section>

<section class="dn-s"><div class="dn-w dn-serve"><div><p class="dn-ey">Kitchen rules</p><h2>Four rules for leftovers</h2><ul class="dn-rules"><li><b>Two hours.</b> Cool cooked food and get it into the fridge within two hours of cooking.</li><li><b>Shallow containers.</b> Food cools faster spread thin than piled deep.</li><li><b>Label it.</b> Write the day on the lid. Memory is not reliable by Thursday.</li><li><b>Steaming hot.</b> Reheat once, until it is piping hot all the way through.</li></ul></div><figure><img src="https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1773958731570-f799fe0c015a?auto=format&fit=crop&w=600&h=640&q=75" alt="person serving a meal with soup and small dishes" width="600" height="640" loading="lazy"></figure></div></section>

<section class="dn-s dn-butter"><div class="dn-w dn-fq"><p class="dn-ey dn-c">Asked at the table</p><h2 class="dn-c">Dinner questions</h2><div class="dn-faq"><details open><summary>How many side dishes should a dinner have?</summary><p>Two is the comfortable number for an everyday dinner: one starchy side and one vegetable. For guests, add a third that is cold or raw, such as a salad, so that not everything needs the hob at the last minute.</p></details><details><summary>How much meat should I buy per person?</summary><p>Allow about 150 to 180 grams of boneless meat or fish per adult, or 300 to 350 grams if it is on the bone. Children usually eat about half an adult portion.</p></details><details><summary>How long do leftovers keep in the fridge?</summary><p>Most cooked dishes keep for two to three days if cooled and refrigerated within two hours. Cooked rice is the exception: cool it quickly and use it within a day.</p></details><details><summary>Can I reheat leftovers more than once?</summary><p>It is safest to reheat only once. Take out just the portion you will eat, heat it until it is steaming hot all the way through, and return the rest to the fridge untouched.</p></details><details><summary>What goes with a rich main course?</summary><p>Something sharp, fresh or crisp. A lemony salad, quick-pickled vegetables or plain steamed greens cut through richness far better than another creamy or buttery dish.</p></details><details><summary>How much rice or pasta per person?</summary><p>About 75 grams of dried rice or 90 to 100 grams of dried pasta per adult as a main, and roughly two thirds of that when it is a side dish.</p></details></div></div></section>

<section class="dn-nl"><div class="dn-w"><h2>The Dinette Card</h2><p>One main, two sides and a leftover idea, sent on the first Sunday of every month.</p><a class="btn" href="mailto:hello@dinette.com?subject=The%20Dinette%20Card&amp;body=Please%20subscribe%20me.">Subscribe by email</a></div></section>

